extends Node3D
## The presentation layer (ARCHITECTURE.md §5) for the data-driven production
## chain `sim_core::World` builds internally — rendered per docs/UI_UX.md:
## full 3D voxel models at the §2 32x32x32 tile scale, viewed through a
## FIXED orthographic isometric camera (§1), lit in real time with shadows,
## ambient occlusion and glow (§4), in a grounded industrial-factory palette
## (§3/§7).
##
## Milestone 5 (Economy MVP, ARCHITECTURE.md §9 item 5) added a minimal money
## + contract-status HUD readout, read every frame from `economy_snapshot_json`.
## It renders as UI_UX.md §5's diegetic clipboard — a paper-toned panel with
## checkbox-style tick marks for delivery progress, its own distinct
## PanelContainer rather than folded into the generic dark HUD panel (see
## `_build_contract_panel`). Since contracts became `data/contracts.ron`
## content it shows ALL of them at once, one docket card each on the one
## board, because GAMEPLAY.md §6's diversification pressure only reaches the
## player if every concurrent obligation is visible together.
##
## **The world starts EMPTY and the player builds it** (the "Real Placement"
## epic). This script no longer learns a chain's shape once from
## `chain_layout_json` — there is no chain, and nothing is built for free. It
## re-reads `world_layout_json` and rebuilds its whole "World" subtree after
## every place, remove and rotate, draws each tile at the facing the player
## gave it, and carries a real build mode: a translucent ghost that follows
## the cursor and says whether the click will work, **R** to turn things,
## **X** to take them back. `chunk_snapshot_json`/`dirty_chunk_ids` still
## drive what MOVES (items on belts, machines starting and stopping) per tick.
## Nothing about the factory is hardcoded here, and nothing here reads a
## belt's gap array or a machine's raw `TimedProcess`. Godot still draws what
## the sim says exists — the difference is that what exists is now the
## player's doing.
##
## Milestone 6 (Fluids) adds the pipe run: pipe tiles and the liquid inside
## them are built and sized entirely from `fluid_snapshot_json` (see
## `_build_fluid_network` for why that call rather than the chunk snapshots),
## and a pipe answers the same hover tooltip a machine does.
##
## The "Machines improvement" milestone's ticket 4 is the presentation half
## of tickets 1-3. Machines are no longer one generic model on one tile: the
## kind and the tile FOOTPRINT the simulation reports pick the model and size
## its placement (see MACHINE_MODELS / `_footprint_centre`), so the 2x2
## cooking vat genuinely covers its four tiles; the chain's hopper is drawn
## as a real building with the product level inside it tracking its live fill
## (`_update_hopper`, built the same way the pipe run's liquid already was);
## and the one hover mechanism resolves any tile of a multi-tile machine, and
## the hopper, alongside the machines and pipes it already covered.
##
## The delivery-dock ticket's presentation half ends the line properly. The
## chain used to be emptied by a keypress — **C** issued `collect_output`,
## which lifted finished cases straight out of the last machine's buffer —
## and the tile past the packer did not exist. Now the simulation lays one
## more ordinary belt there and stands a `dock` building at its end that
## ships into the economy's shared delivery pool every tick by itself, so
## **C** and its control hint are gone and `_build_dock` draws what replaced
## them. The goods standing on the dock's deck come from
## `economy_snapshot_json`'s `delivery_stock`, NOT from the dock's own buffer,
## which is a pass-through and always reads empty — see DOCK_ASSET.
##
## **Belts, splitters, pipes and the items riding them are INSTANCED** (the
## pipes & conveyors overhaul's ticket 4): one `MultiMesh` per (asset,
## material class) with a transform per tile, and one pool per item asset,
## instead of a node per tile and a pooled node per item. Machines and
## buildings keep their nodes — they carry lamps, gauges and fill bodies. The
## "Instanced tiles and items" section explains the split and the three
## seams later tickets build on (frame sets for belt animation, a caller-
## supplied yaw per tile for corners, one item-positioning function).
##
## Assets come from `res://voxels/`, staged there by `cargo xtask` from
## `assets/voxels/models/` (generated by `cargo run -p voxgen`). Each asset is
## one mesh per material class, so steel can be shinier than concrete and
## status lamps can actually emit — UI_UX.md §4's explicit metal exception.
##
## Two view modes, toggled with TAB: ISOMETRIC (this doc's §1 fixed
## orthographic camera, pan/zoom only) and FIRST_PERSON (a free-look
## perspective "walk" camera, not part of UI_UX.md — this project has no
## player entity or collision geometry, so it's a fly-cam: no gravity, no
## walls stop you). Both modes reuse the same single `Camera3D` node,
## switching its projection/position/orientation rather than swapping nodes,
## so "exactly one Camera3D" stays true regardless of mode.
##
## The save/main-menu epic's ticket 2 added the two things a session needs
## around the factory: this scene is now entered FROM `menu.tscn` (carrying
## the player's New Game / Load choice on the `GameSession` autoload) and it
## has a pause menu on **ESC** with the way back out — save, quit to menu,
## quit game — plus a three-minute autosave. All the file handling lives in
## `game_session.gd`, because `sim_core` deliberately has none.

## Where saves live and what they may be called — the same static helpers
## `menu.gd` reads the list with, so the screen that writes a save and the
## screen that loads one cannot disagree about either.
const GameSaves = preload("res://game_session.gd")
## The voxel loader, shared with the title screen (see the Assets section).
const VoxelAssets = preload("res://voxel_assets.gd")
## The hall's light and backdrop, shared with the title screen.
const FactoryEnvironment = preload("res://factory_environment.gd")

# --- Scale ---------------------------------------------------------------
# UI_UX.md §2 fixes one world tile at 32x32x32 voxels. The .vox importer's
# default Scale option turns one voxel into 0.1 world units, so a tile is
# 3.2 units across and every asset shares that unit automatically.
const VOXELS_PER_TILE := 32
const VOXEL_UNIT := 0.1
const TILE := VOXELS_PER_TILE * VOXEL_UNIT

# Heights below are stated in voxels and converted, so they stay readable
# against the generators in assets/voxels/generators/assets.rs.
const FLOOR_VOXELS := 3 # floor_tile's slab thickness
const BELT_SURFACE_VOXELS := 10 # top of belt's running surface
const FLOOR_Y := FLOOR_VOXELS * VOXEL_UNIT
const BELT_SURFACE_Y := FLOOR_Y + BELT_SURFACE_VOXELS * VOXEL_UNIT

# How far past the chain the ISOMETRIC camera's auto-framing considers —
# kept small and deliberately separate from FLOOR_GROUND_MARGIN below, or a
# bigger floor would also zoom the default isometric view out to fit it.
const FLOOR_MARGIN_X := 2
const FLOOR_MARGIN_Z := 2
# How far the floor MESH actually extends past the chain — much larger than
# the framing margin above, so flying around in first-person doesn't reveal
# a "floating island" edge a few steps past the machines. Purely a ground
# plane; the isometric camera never zooms out to show all of it.
const FLOOR_GROUND_MARGIN_X := 24
const FLOOR_GROUND_MARGIN_Z := 24
# How much floor the isometric camera frames when the world is EMPTY — the
# state a new game now starts in, where there is no factory to frame. Enough
# tiles to lay a first line across without immediately panning.
const EMPTY_WORLD_FRAME_TILES := 14

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The factory floor's variants (the floor redesign)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `assets.rs`'s factory-floor section builds four slab models; which one a
# tile gets is decided HERE, from the tile's own coordinates, because that is
# the only place the coordinates exist. The rule is a pure function of
# (tx, tz) — no randomness and no stored state — so the same world always
# draws the same floor, a reloaded save is not repaved, and the floor does
# not shuffle itself every time `_build_floor` runs (which is every time
# anything is placed or removed).
#
# Frequencies are deliberately low. The plain slab is the floor; the rest are
# incident. A variant that shows up on every tenth tile stops being an
# incident and becomes a chequerboard, which is the failure mode this whole
# redesign exists to fix.
const FLOOR_ASSET_PLAIN := "floor_tile"
const FLOOR_ASSET_WORN := "floor_tile_worn"
const FLOOR_ASSET_PATCH := "floor_tile_patch"
const FLOOR_ASSET_DRAIN := "floor_tile_drain"
## Trench drains run in unbroken LINES, one tile row deep, every this many
## rows — a drain is plumbing, and plumbing runs somewhere. Scattering single
## drain tiles by hash would read as potholes.
const FLOOR_DRAIN_ROW_PITCH := 9
## Offset of those rows, chosen so no drain crosses tile row 0 — the origin
## is where an empty world is framed and where a player lays their first
## belt, and it should be plain floor.
const FLOOR_DRAIN_ROW_PHASE := 4
## One tile in this many is worn, and one in this many is a repair patch
## (of the tiles that are not drains).
const FLOOR_WORN_IN := 7
const FLOOR_PATCH_IN := 97

const TICK_HZ := 30.0
## Never advance more than this many simulation ticks in one frame, and drop
## whatever backlog is left rather than paying it back later.
##
## Found while reviewing this ticket's junctions: the catch-up loop in
## `_process` was unbounded, so one slow frame — a cold shader cache on the
## first frames of a run is the reliable one — silently fast-forwarded the
## factory by however many ticks that frame was worth. Two identical
## screenshot runs of the same populate sequence photographed visibly
## different factories (one with a jammed first belt, one with it drained),
## which makes a rendered scene useless as a before/after check. It is also
## just wrong on its own terms: a rendering hitch should cost you frames, not
## silently run the simulation forward while nothing is drawn.
const MAX_TICKS_PER_FRAME := 8
const VOX_DIR := "res://voxels/"

# --- Fluid pipes (milestone 6; the shape family is the pipes & conveyors
# overhaul's ticket 6, docs/PIPES_AND_CONVEYORS_PLAN.md) --------------------
##
## A pipe tile is drawn from three kinds of part, composed here from the
## `links` the simulation puts on every pipe placement (`links.connections`,
## one entry per joined side, with the KIND of thing on that side) — never
## from geometry this file works out for itself:
##
##   * a SHAPE, decided by how many sides connect and how they lie: one is
##     an end, two opposite a straight, two adjacent a corner, three a tee,
##     four a cross (`_pipe_shape_for`);
##   * a FLUID BODY per shape, one voxel tall and scaled to the live level
##     exactly as before (`_fluid_body_for`);
##   * a FITTING on every side whose connection is a machine or a tank —
##     the pipe's half of that joint — overlaid on the shape as its own
##     instance, turned to that side (`_pipe_fitting_for`).
##
## Every shape is authored with its faces where `assets/voxels/generators/
## assets.rs`'s pipe section says (a corner joins +X and NORTH; a tee +X,
## -X and north; an end is open on -X only), and Godot's job is to find the
## quarter-turn count that lands those faces on the tile's connected sides.
## That count is what `_stage_tile_instance` is handed as the yaw, so a pipe
## tile's yaw is a property of its connections, NOT of its `direction`: a
## straight always keeps its one joint flange on the same side of every tile
## along an axis (west on an east-west run, north on a north-south one), so
## two neighbours can never stack their flanges on a shared boundary — which
## a per-tile `direction` (east on one, west on the next) did. The
## placement's `direction` decides only which axis a pipe joined to NOTHING
## lies along; every other pipe's axes are decided by what it is joined to.
const PIPE_ASSET := "pipe"
const PIPE_CORNER_ASSET := "pipe_corner"
const PIPE_TEE_ASSET := "pipe_tee"
const PIPE_CROSS_ASSET := "pipe_cross"
const PIPE_END_ASSET := "pipe_end"
const PIPE_FITTING_MACHINE_ASSET := "pipe_fitting_machine"
const PIPE_FITTING_TANK_ASSET := "pipe_fitting_tank"
## The liquid body for each shape, keyed by the shape's asset. One per
## shape rather than one slab stretched about, so a corner's liquid turns
## with its bore and an end's stops short of its cap (no Godot-side trim).
const PIPE_FLUID_ASSETS := {
	"pipe": "pipe_fluid",
	"pipe_corner": "pipe_fluid_corner",
	"pipe_tee": "pipe_fluid_tee",
	"pipe_cross": "pipe_fluid_cross",
	"pipe_end": "pipe_fluid_end",
}
const PIPE_FLUID_ASSET := "pipe_fluid"
## The hazard clamp overlaid on a run the simulation cannot name, and the
## two `FluidSnapshot.naming` verdicts that put it there (issue #54). The
## other two — `carries` and `unplumbed` — are not faults: the first works,
## and the second is what a run looks like while it is still being laid.
const PIPE_FAULT_ASSET := "pipe_fault"
const PIPE_FAULT_NAMINGS := {"unnameable": true, "ambiguous": true}
# Where the liquid sits inside the pipe, stated in voxels against
# assets/voxels/generators/assets.rs's PIPE_BORE_Z0 / PIPE_BORE_HEIGHT — the
# same mirroring BELT_SURFACE_VOXELS already does for the belt. The fluid
# model is deliberately ONE voxel tall (see its generator doc), so scaling it
# by `pressure * PIPE_BORE_HEIGHT_VOXELS` grows a liquid column from the bore
# floor up, with no pivot arithmetic. Unchanged by the shape family (ticket
# 3 kept every shape's bore on the same floor and crown).
const PIPE_BORE_FLOOR_VOXELS := 12
const PIPE_BORE_HEIGHT_VOXELS := 7
const PIPE_BORE_FLOOR_Y := FLOOR_Y + PIPE_BORE_FLOOR_VOXELS * VOXEL_UNIT
## Below this fill fraction the liquid body is hidden outright rather than
## drawn as a sub-voxel sliver that would z-fight the bore floor.
const FLUID_MIN_VISIBLE_PRESSURE := 0.02
## The `kind` values a pipe's `links.connections` can carry that are not
## another pipe: each gets a fitting on that side.
const PIPE_LINK_MACHINE := "machine"
const PIPE_LINK_TANK := "tank"
const PIPE_LINK_PIPE := "pipe"

## Presentation-only tint per fluid id, exactly like ITEM_MODELS above: the
## fluid ids themselves come from data/recipes.ron, and a fluid with no entry
## here still renders (in a neutral pale blue) rather than vanishing. The
## voxel model is near-white so this multiplies cleanly over it.
const FLUID_TINTS := {
	# `palette.ron`'s `vinegar_amber` (#AD6B23), and equal to it on purpose:
	# the jug of stock in the market and the liquid in the run are the same
	# substance, so they are the same number in the one file that owns
	# colour. It used to be a pale gold picked here and nowhere else, which
	# read as cooking oil in a stainless pipe — vinegar is amber, and an
	# amber that dark is also further from `hazard_yellow` than a gold is,
	# so a full pipe no longer reads as signage.
	"vinegar": Color(0.678, 0.420, 0.137),
	# The two fluids the chain rework put in the pipes: the mixer's uncooked
	# blend and the vat's finished ketchup. They are deliberately a shade
	# apart rather than one red — the two runs stand a few tiles from each
	# other on the same row, and the whole point of piping the middle of the
	# chain is that the player can see the product change as it goes down the
	# line. These match `palette.ron`'s own `liquid_mix` and `liquid_ketchup`
	# (the pails' colours) so a fluid keeps its identity wherever it appears.
	"ketchup_mix": Color(0.69, 0.23, 0.15),
	"ketchup": Color(0.77, 0.10, 0.05),
	# The BBQ branch's three fluids (issue #68). They have no `liquid_*`
	# entry in `palette.ron` to mirror, and deliberately so — that file
	# records that nothing carries BBQ in an open pail, so the only voxels
	# ever painted `bbq_brown` are a bottle's contents and a case's print,
	# and the fluid in the run is this number instead. They are picked
	# against `bbq_brown` (#4E2419) rather than from nowhere: the run is the
	# same substance as the bottle, lifted because a bore is a shadowed
	# surface and a tint that dark reads as an EMPTY pipe rather than a full
	# one, which is the failure `vinegar`'s note above already records from
	# the other end (too pale reads as water).
	#
	# The three have to stay tellable apart from each other AND from the
	# ketchup pair a few tiles south, so they move along two axes the way
	# `ketchup_mix`/`ketchup` move along one: the cook DARKENS (the mix is
	# ketchup cut with molasses and spice; the vat cooks it down) and the
	# whole branch is BROWNER than the ketchup run is red. `liquid_smoke` is
	# the odd one out on purpose — it is a flavouring, not a sauce, so it is
	# a smoky desaturated grey-brown that reads as "not food" beside five
	# saturated ones, and it is well clear of `hazard_yellow` and of
	# `vinegar`'s amber, the other raw fluid a tank feeds.
	"bbq_mix": Color(0.48, 0.25, 0.15),
	"bbq": Color(0.33, 0.17, 0.11),
	"liquid_smoke": Color(0.36, 0.32, 0.26),
}
const FLUID_TINT_FALLBACK := Color(0.60, 0.79, 0.86)

## --- The belt family (the pipes & conveyors overhaul, ticket 5) -----------
##
## A belt tile's SHAPE is derived from the `links` the simulation puts on
## every belt placement (ARCHITECTURE.md §4, the plan's "Shape rules"),
## never from an adjacency scan of this file's own: a tile fed from behind is
## straight, a tile fed from one flank is a corner turning toward its
## facing, a tile fed from two sides is a merge (drawn straight), a tile fed
## by nothing is a line start and one handing to nothing is a line end (both
## drawn straight). `_belt_shape_for` is the whole rule and is pure, so a
## test can hand it any `links` it likes.
##
## Each shape is a FOUR-FRAME SET (`belt_f0..f3`, `belt_corner_left_f0..f3`,
## `belt_corner_right_f0..f3`, generated by `assets/voxels/generators/
## assets.rs`'s `belt_shape`); `_belt_frame_for_tick` picks the frame the
## whole scene shows. Every model is authored with its output on +X and is
## spun by the placement's own facing like any other asset (UI_UX.md §2b
## point 18) — a corner needs no yaw of its own, because `belt_corner_left`
## is authored fed from the north, which is exactly the flank one quarter
## turn anticlockwise from east, and that relation survives the spin.
##
## **There are no belt-side junction variants any more.** `belt_inlet` and
## `belt_outlet` (a static dead-plate on the tile before and after a
## machine, authored east-only and falling back to the plain tile on three
## facings out of four) were retired by ticket 2 and their models deleted
## here: the plain tile carries the junction and the machine's own infeed
## hood and outfeed doorway do the rest, at every facing (UI_UX.md §2b
## point 23). A belt now looks connected to a machine, hopper or dock
## whichever way it faces, because there is nothing facing-specific left on
## the belt's side of the boundary.
const BELT_SHAPE_STRAIGHT := "straight"
const BELT_SHAPE_CORNER_LEFT := "corner_left"
const BELT_SHAPE_CORNER_RIGHT := "corner_right"
const BELT_SHAPE_MERGE := "merge"
const BELT_SHAPE_START := "start"
const BELT_SHAPE_END := "end"
## Shape -> the asset family it draws with (frame `k` is `"<family>_f<k>"`).
## Four of the six shapes share the straight family: there is no separate
## merge, start or end model, by ticket 2's decision.
const BELT_SHAPE_FAMILIES := {
	BELT_SHAPE_STRAIGHT: "belt",
	BELT_SHAPE_CORNER_LEFT: "belt_corner_left",
	BELT_SHAPE_CORNER_RIGHT: "belt_corner_right",
	BELT_SHAPE_MERGE: "belt",
	BELT_SHAPE_START: "belt",
	BELT_SHAPE_END: "belt",
}
## Animation frames per belt shape. Mirrors `voxgen`'s `BELT_FRAMES`;
## change one, change both.
const BELT_FRAMES := 4
## Ticks an item takes to cross one belt tile. Mirrors
## `sim_core/src/sim/belt.rs`'s `BELT_TICKS_PER_TILE`; the surface pattern
## is driven from it so the rubber moves at exactly item speed.
const BELT_TICKS_PER_TILE := 50

# --- Machines & the hopper (the "Machines improvement" milestone) ----------
## Which voxel model draws each machine KIND from `data/machines.ron`.
## Presentation-only, exactly like ITEM_MODELS below: the kind ids come from
## that data file (via `chain_layout_json`'s `machine_kind`), and a kind with
## no entry here still renders — as the generic `machine` model — rather than
## vanishing. That fallback is deliberate and load-bearing: adding a kind to
## `data/machines.ron` must never require a Godot edit before the chain will
## draw.
##
## Since the "Visual overhaul round 2" epic's ticket 2 **every kind
## `data/machines.ron` currently defines has a model of its own**, so the
## fallback is now a safety net rather than something the shipped chain
## relies on. It used to catch `bottler` and `packer`, which meant the last
## two stages both rendered as another copy of the generic housing — the
## project owner's review named exactly that ("some machines are still
## missing assets or just reuse assets (bottling machine)").
const MACHINE_MODELS := {
	"crusher": "machine_crusher",
	"mixer": "machine_mixer",
	"cooking_vat": "machine_cooking_vat",
	"bottler": "machine_bottler",
	"packer": "machine_packer",
	# Epic B's conveyor splitter. It is not a processing machine at all — it
	# has no recipe, it is priced with the belts and it carries product at
	# belt speed — but it has a footprint in `data/machines.ron` like every
	# placed thing, and this table is keyed on that file's kinds, so it is
	# where its model belongs. See `_build_splitter` for the live half.
	"splitter": "machine_splitter",
	# The chain's exit (the delivery-dock ticket). It is not a `chain_layout_json`
	# stage — nothing in `data/recipes.ron` names it — so `_build_chain` never
	# looks it up; `_build_dock` and the build menu's `_shop_asset_for` both do,
	# and they find it here rather than in a table of their own.
	"dock": "machine_dock",
}
const MACHINE_MODEL_FALLBACK := "machine"

## Which MOTION OVERLAY draws each kind's moving parts (the machine-animation
## pass; `assets.rs`'s "Machine motion" section is the other half of this).
##
## A machine's body no longer contains the parts that move: the crusher's
## flywheel spokes and roll teeth, the mixer's ribbon, the bottler's nozzles
## and capping chuck, the packer's taping carriage are all in a separate
## model with the same footprint, drawn in `MACHINE_FRAMES` arrangements.
## This scene stands all of them on the machine's tiles as siblings of the
## body and shows exactly one — the belt family's own frame mechanism
## (UI_UX.md §2b point 23) applied to something that is not a tile.
##
## Keyed on kind and fallen through exactly like MACHINE_MODELS, for the same
## load-bearing reason: a kind added to `data/machines.ron` must draw, and
## must visibly RUN, without a Godot edit first. The fallback overlay is a
## drive spider on the generic housing's crown.
const MACHINE_MOTION_MODELS := {
	"crusher": "machine_crusher_motion",
	"mixer": "machine_mixer_motion",
	"cooking_vat": "machine_cooking_vat_motion",
	"bottler": "machine_bottler_motion",
	"packer": "machine_packer_motion",
}
const MACHINE_MOTION_FALLBACK := "machine_motion"

## Frames in one machine's motion cycle. Mirrors `voxgen`'s `MACHINE_FRAMES`
## (`assets.rs`); change one, change both.
const MACHINE_FRAMES := 4
## Ticks one full motion cycle takes, for EVERY kind.
##
## Uniform on purpose, and it is the one number here that is a judgement
## rather than a fact about the models. A machine's own process length would
## be the obvious driver — the crusher's 80 ticks against the vat's 240 — but
## that reads as two factories running at different speeds rather than as one
## factory with a slow cook in it, and the cook already states its length
## through the product backing up behind it. So the pace says "working", not
## "working this fast", and the belt is what carries speed.
##
## 30 ticks at TICK_HZ 30 is four frame changes a second, against the belt
## surface's 2.4 (`BELT_TICKS_PER_TILE` 50 over `BELT_FRAMES` 4). Judged by
## the numbers rather than by eye, the same way the belt's march was: a
## mechanism should read as busier than the rubber it feeds, and a fifth of a
## second a frame is the chunky end of UI_UX.md §6 rather than a blur.
const MACHINE_TICKS_PER_CYCLE := 30

## --- The machine load readout (the machine-load epic, ticket 3; moved
## --- off the floor and into the hover panel on 2026-09-12) ---------------
##
## A machine's load is the simulation's own verdict on its last ten seconds
## (`machine_load_snapshots_json`'s `status`: `working`, `starved` or
## `blocked`) — this file never re-derives it from the counts. It is READ
## BY HOVERING the machine: the tooltip's load line leads with a status
## glyph and word, and the load bar under it takes the status colour. It
## used to stand in the world as well, as an andon stack light beside every
## machine; those poles were removed after a playtest (the owner did not
## like how they looked), and the hover panel is now the one place the
## status lives. The glyphs are shapes as well as colours (UI_UX.md §7),
## and this table is also the order the sim spells the statuses, so it is
## the one place the words live.
const LOAD_STATUS_GLYPHS := {"working": "●", "starved": "▲", "blocked": "■"}
## How often the load is re-read from the simulation, in ticks. Load is
## polled rather than dirtied (ARCHITECTURE.md §4: it changes on every
## machine every tick), and one bucket of the sim's window (`LOAD_BUCKET_TICKS`,
## 30) is the finest grain the verdict can change at, so polling faster
## would only re-read the same answer. One poll a second at TICK_HZ.
const LOAD_POLL_TICKS := 30

## Which machine kinds draw a live PROGRESS GAUGE beside their model, and
## which asset that gauge's moving bar is (ARCHITECTURE.md §9 milestone 7).
##
## Keyed on kind, like MACHINE_MODELS, and deliberately sparse — **empty, as
## the shipped content now stands.** A gauge is not a thing every machine
## should have: the crusher's whole process is 80 ticks, well under two
## seconds, so a bar tracking it would be a flicker, and five flickering bars
## along the line would be noise rather than information. The rule this table
## encodes is that a gauge earns its place when a process runs long enough
## that "how far through is it" stops being answerable by the status lamp —
## which is a question of TIMESCALE, not of importance.
##
